Twist TODO List
================

SCP/SFTP (src/ssh, over foxtrot)

  - Ask foxtrot for the number of requests outstanding in an SFTP client.
    gf_sftp_client_pending is the bytes queued, so twist counts its own (sftp.c
    sent () and inflight).
  - statvfs, and the other commands the sftp tool has, as -Q commands;
    --create-file-mode and the directory mode.
  - Quote commands for scp URLs.
  - Certificates are found only as KEY-cert.pub beside a key file; there is no
    option to name a certificate elsewhere (ssh's CertificateFile).
  - -C and -T completion of the URL for the schemes other than sftp and scp.
  - Keyboard-interactive questions that need a person, such as a one-time
    code: twist answers only a single hidden question with the password.  A
    prompt on the terminal would need a way for the application to be asked.
  - Passphrase-protected OpenSSH-format private keys.  Blocked on bcrypt-pbkdf
    being available in libgcrypt; it is deliberately not taken from anywhere
    else.  An ssh-agent holding the key is the workaround, and works.


FTPS (src/ftp/ftp.c)

  - Session reuse needs a TLS 1.3 ticket from the control connection.  Against
    a TLS 1.2 server, or one that gave none, the data connection does a full
    handshake, and a server that requires reuse then refuses it.
  - A data connection that ends without a close_notify is taken as the end of
    the file, as it is for ftp, so a truncated transfer is not noticed (the
    size in the 150 reply is parsed and not used for this).
  - No CCC, and no PROT C or PROT S choice: PROT P or nothing.


Misc

  - --connect-timeout for ldap covers the lookup and the TCP connect only; a
    server that takes the connection and does not answer the bind is waited for
    until --max-time.  What curl does for ldap is not known: the curl it was
    compared with has no LDAP.
  - STARTTLS for ldap (curl has it through OpenLDAP only).  A user name and
    password for ldap without the s go in the clear.


https proxies (src/engine/tunnel.c, masque.c)

  - Templated CONNECT-TCP (draft-ietf-httpbis-connect-tcp), and
    --proxy-template-tcp for it, when it is an RFC.


Progress meter (see HACKING)

  - An upload is counted when the body is handed to the connection (HTTP,
    SMTP, MQTT, TFTP), not when the socket has taken it; sftp counts when the
    server has acknowledged a write.  A large POST on a slow link is at 100%
    sooner than it should be.
  - No size is known for POP3, LDAP, DICT, Gopher, WebSocket, MQTT or a
    directory listing.  For FTP the size is the number the reply to RETR may
    carry; asking the server (SIZE) would give it more often.
  - An HTTP response that is coded (--compressed) is counted by its bytes on
    the wire, which is what Content-Length counts; TWISTINFO_SIZE_DOWNLOAD is
    the decoded.
  - sftp and scp ask for the size of the file (one more round trip) only when
    the meter is on.


DNS (src/dns, RFC 8305)

  - HTTP/3 and QUIC under a proxy use one address (IPv4 if there is one) and
    are not raced; nor is a TCP connection through a proxy, whose own address
    is looked up with the one-address resolver.
  - The cache holds addresses for the sink only when the HTTPS record is not
    wanted, so an http(s) lookup is never cached.
  - DoQ and DoH send their queries one after another (three round trips for an
    http URL); DoT, TCP and UDP send them together.  DoH can only be pipelined
    over HTTP/2, and DoQ needs a stream for each, since every id is 0.
  - RFC 10029: the types in one message, the extra ones in an MQTYPE-Query
    option, and the server says which it included.  None of 1.1.1.1, 8.8.8.8
    and 9.9.9.9 does yet (tried), so the queries go out separately in one
    flight.  gumdrop has the codec (dns/DnsMultiQType.java); a server that
    answers with MQTYPE-Response could be remembered and sent one message.
  - A UDP query is not sent again when it is lost: it is waited for until the
    transfer's timeout.
  - With the default order a discovery query (DDR) is made before every lookup
    that is not in the cache, and its outcome is not remembered.
  - What has not been answered 2 s after the first address is given up on (the
    lookup is complete then): a very slow nameserver loses its other types.

Environment

  - A configuration file.
  - A script that sets CURL_CA_BUNDLE gets the system's authorities; whether
    to read it is undecided.
  - SSL_CERT_DIR is read as a directory of PEM files by name (.pem, .crt, .cer
    or a c_rehash name), not through OpenSSL's rules.


Tests

  - Fuzzing covers the parsers in the library (see HACKING).  Not reached: the
    command line's own parsing (main.c: options, the speed and time arguments,
    --write-out), which is static there; the DNS cache and resolver state
    machines; the SFTP engine's use of foxtrot, which could be driven by a
    fuzzed server session; the Set-Cookie/HSTS persistence; and the file
    scheme.  A fuzzer of a whole transfer, with a scripted peer on a
    socketpair, would reach the connection and handler glue.
  - The unit tests are meant to do no I/O, not even loopback.  These still
    open sockets of their own: test-env (a UDP socket), test-resolve (a fake
    nameserver on UDP), test-race, test-origin and test-runtime-threads
    (listening TCP servers on threads), and test-telnet.  Each should be
    driven through a mock endpoint (as test-reactor does with socketpair, which
    is still a kernel object, so a pure in-memory transport would be better) or
    move to integration/.  cli-parallel.sh also runs servers.

Runtime

  - The storage pool has two threads, shared by the file handler's reads and
    the SSH key and known_hosts loads.  Letting go of a file transfer no longer
    waits for its worker, but a read that never comes back still holds one of
    the two until the process ends, and two such reads hold up every file
    transfer and SSH load behind them.  A larger pool only moves the limit; a
    read that can be given up on would need a thread to be cancelled, or a
    different way to read.
  - An io_uring poll backend.


Protocols not yet done

  - SMB/SMBS.
  - RTMP/RTMPS.
  - NTLM and Negotiate, only after an explicit dependency decision.
